# Twitter/X API Core

Lux includes a Twitter/X API v2 client for OAuth 2.0 PKCE, post management,
user interactions, media upload, rate-limit handling, and error normalization.

## Setup

Configure a bearer token when you want the client to use application
configuration instead of passing `:access_token` per call:

```elixir
config :lux, :api_keys,
  twitter_bearer: System.fetch_env!("TWITTER_BEARER_TOKEN")
```

## OAuth 2.0 PKCE

```elixir
alias Lux.Integrations.Twitter.Client

pkce = Client.pkce_pair()

Client.authorization_url(%{
  client_id: "client-id",
  redirect_uri: "https://example.com/callback",
  state: "csrf-token",
  code_verifier: pkce.verifier
})
```

Exchange an authorization code or refresh token:

```elixir
Client.token_request(:authorization_code, %{
  client_id: "client-id",
  client_secret: "client-secret",
  redirect_uri: "https://example.com/callback",
  code: "returned-code",
  code_verifier: pkce.verifier
})

Client.token_request(:refresh_token, %{
  client_id: "client-id",
  client_secret: "client-secret",
  refresh_token: "refresh-token"
})
```

## Posts

```elixir
Client.create_tweet(%{text: "Hello from Lux"}, access_token: "user-token")

Client.create_tweet(
  %{
    text: "Reply with media",
    reply_to_tweet_id: "123",
    media_ids: ["1146654567674912769"]
  },
  access_token: "user-token"
)

Client.quote_tweet("Adding context", "123", access_token: "user-token")

Client.edit_tweet("123", %{text: "Edited text"}, access_token: "user-token")

Client.create_thread(["first", "second", "third"], access_token: "user-token")

Client.delete_tweet("123", access_token: "user-token")
```

## Users And Follows

```elixir
Client.get_me(%{user_fields: ["username", "public_metrics"]}, access_token: "user-token")
Client.get_user("2244994945", %{user_fields: ["username"]}, access_token: "user-token")
Client.get_user_by_username("xdevelopers", %{}, access_token: "user-token")

Client.follow_user("source-user-id", "target-user-id", access_token: "user-token")
Client.unfollow_user("source-user-id", "target-user-id", access_token: "user-token")
```

## Chunked Media Upload

```elixir
Client.media_upload(
  :init,
  %{total_bytes: 1_048_576, media_type: "video/mp4", media_category: "tweet_video"},
  access_token: "user-token"
)

Client.media_upload(
  :append,
  %{media_id: "1880028106020515840", segment_index: 0, media: {:file, "/tmp/chunk.mp4"}},
  access_token: "user-token"
)

Client.media_upload(:finalize, %{media_id: "1880028106020515840"}, access_token: "user-token")
Client.media_upload(:status, %{media_id: "1880028106020515840"}, access_token: "user-token")
```

## Rate Limits And Errors

```elixir
{:ok, %{body: body, rate_limit: limit}} =
  Client.request(:get, "/2/users/me", access_token: "user-token", with_rate_limit: true)

{:error, {:rate_limited, limit}} =
  Client.request(:get, "/2/users/me", access_token: "expired-token")
```

`Lux.Integrations.Twitter.classify_error/3` normalizes common API responses
such as invalid tokens, forbidden requests, missing resources, validation
errors, conflicts, and rate limits.

## Local Benchmark

Use the benchmark helper for local planning/encoding cost without network calls:

```elixir
Lux.Integrations.Twitter.Client.benchmark(fn ->
  Lux.Integrations.Twitter.Client.authorization_url(%{
    client_id: "client-id",
    redirect_uri: "https://example.com/callback",
    state: "state",
    code_verifier: "verifier"
  })
end)
```
